From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 06:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mali-r38p1: find the context's task without a pid-namespace
 lookup

kbase_context_common_init() records current->tgid - the global id - in
kctx->tgid, then looks the task up again with find_get_pid(kctx->tgid).
find_get_pid() resolves a number in the caller's own pid namespace, not the
global one. So for any process inside a container with a pid namespace of
its own the lookup fails, and creating a GPU context returns -ESRCH
("Failed to get pid pointer for ..."), or it finds an unrelated task that
happens to have that number inside the namespace. On LuneOS this is
Waydroid: Android runs in an LXC container with its own pid namespace, so
its EGL cannot initialise and Android never finishes booting. Processes
outside a namespace - the LuneOS compositor - are not affected.

The task wanted is the caller's thread group leader, which is what the pid
lookup on the tgid returned. Take current->group_leader directly: the same
task, no lookup, no namespace. kctx->tgid itself is left alone; everywhere
else it is only compared against other global ids or logged.

The same fix as the Zinwa Q25's for r32p1 (meta-zinwa 0004), where Android
in Waydroid reached sys.boot_completed with it. The OOM notifier in
mali_kbase_device.c does a second find_get_pid(kctx->pid), but only to
print the task's name, and prints "[null task]" when it misses; it is left
as it is.

Checked host-side against the BraX3 kernel (5.15.209-android13-8-gdd511ad2a748):
builds without warnings, and against the stock mali_kbase_mt6835.ko from
vendor_dlkm the same vermagic and depends, all 397 imported and 134
exported symbol CRCs identical. Not yet run on a device.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 .../arm/midgard/context/mali_kbase_context.c  | 46 ++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 31 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gpu/gpu_mali/mali_valhall/mali-r38p1/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/context/mali_kbase_context.c b/gpu/gpu_mali/mali_valhall/mali-r38p1/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/context/mali_kbase_context.c
index 2c317d3..9eb39c1 100644
--- a/gpu/gpu_mali/mali_valhall/mali-r38p1/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/context/mali_kbase_context.c
+++ b/gpu/gpu_mali/mali_valhall/mali-r38p1/drivers/gpu/arm/midgard/context/mali_kbase_context.c
@@ -157,37 +157,21 @@ int kbase_context_common_init(struct kbase_context *kctx)
 
 	/* Check if this is a Userspace created context */
 	if (likely(kctx->kfile)) {
-		struct pid *pid_struct;
-
-		rcu_read_lock();
-		pid_struct = find_get_pid(kctx->tgid);
-		if (likely(pid_struct)) {
-			struct task_struct *task = pid_task(pid_struct, PIDTYPE_PID);
-
-			if (likely(task)) {
-				/* Take a reference on the task to avoid slow lookup
-				 * later on from the page allocation loop.
-				 */
-				get_task_struct(task);
-				kctx->task = task;
-			} else {
-				dev_err(kctx->kbdev->dev,
-					"Failed to get task pointer for %s/%d",
-					current->comm, current->pid);
-				err = -ESRCH;
-			}
-
-			put_pid(pid_struct);
-		} else {
-			dev_err(kctx->kbdev->dev,
-				"Failed to get pid pointer for %s/%d",
-				current->comm, current->pid);
-			err = -ESRCH;
-		}
-		rcu_read_unlock();
-
-		if (unlikely(err))
-			return err;
+		/* Take a reference on the task to avoid slow lookup later on
+		 * from the page allocation loop.
+		 *
+		 * The task wanted is the caller's thread group leader. It used
+		 * to be found with find_get_pid(kctx->tgid), but kctx->tgid is
+		 * the global id while find_get_pid() resolves a number in the
+		 * caller's own pid namespace - so from any process in a
+		 * container with a pid namespace of its own the lookup failed
+		 * with -ESRCH ("Failed to get pid pointer"), or found an
+		 * unrelated task that happened to have that number there. No
+		 * GPU context could be created in a container at all.
+		 * group_leader is the same task, reached without a lookup.
+		 */
+		get_task_struct(current->group_leader);
+		kctx->task = current->group_leader;
 
 		/* This merely takes a reference on the mm_struct and not on the
 		 * address space and so won't block the freeing of address space
-- 
